WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.380
Film Courage: I have a William Goldman quote
here and one of his quotes reads “Screenplays

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.370
are structure and that’s all they are.”

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.280
Do you agree with this quote and why do you
think so many screenwriters argue about structure?

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.210
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: Well first

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:18.370
of all…it’s hard to not agree with William
Goldman who was arguably the dean of contemporary

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.430
American screenwriters.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:25.610
I mean an amazing legacy and I never met him
but people who had just said that he was the

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.500
greatest guy.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.900
And so the idea that screenplays are structure,
there is a very real way in which that is

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:33.900
true I think.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:34.910
Ultimately a screenplay is a pre-movie.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:43.540
It’s a template and a guide to make a movie
and so there is a structural component just

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:48.720
like that where it gets budgeted and they
do everything that they have to do with their

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.970
pre-production or production.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:56.680
From a writing standpoint it’s also true
because beginning, middle and end and subplot

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.170
and all of that stuff too.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.250
Why people argue about it?

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:11.899
I think that a lot of it is people want or
are inclined to think about screenplays at

00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:23.079
a surface level like page counts and act breaks
and that sort of thing (stylistic thing).

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:30.079
You can’t have any un-filmables or flashbacks
or voiceover narration, weak writing.

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:41.599
Because that puts the conversation in a realm
where it is pretty easy and in reality to

00:01:41.599 --> 00:01:46.189
write a great script, it’s none of that.

00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:52.919
It’s all about immersing yourself in the
lives of your characters and seeing what emerges

00:01:52.919 --> 00:01:57.569
from that process and that is not easy.

00:01:57.569 --> 00:02:03.689
And there is no golden parachute, secret way
of doing that.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:14.000
Characters are slippery and many of them are
hard to know and figuring out the relationships

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.650
and what they need and what they want, their
conscious goal, unconscious goal, examining

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.680
their personal history which is everything
that has happened to them to find our their

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:30.120
backstory elements which are the key elements
that come forward into the story.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:38.000
That is hard work and that requires trust
and faith that the characters exist and that

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.280
is a conversation that I think people are
afraid of.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:46.380
And so they will argue about story structure
and I’ve seen this flame wars.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:53.200
I wrote a series of blog articles on these
“so-called screenwriting rules.”

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.680
They want to talk about these rules.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.650
There are no rules.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.650
If there were rules, there would be rule books.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:08.040
There are conventions, there are expectations,
there are even paradigms, but not rules.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.530
And so people want to reduce it to rules,
they want to reduce it to formula, they want

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:19.650
to reduce it to specific paradigms, to structure
as plot.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.850
When structure is not just plot.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.940
There is the external world of action and
dialogue which I call the plot line.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.770
That is the realm of the psychical journey.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.150
That is only half the story.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.670
There is the internal world so where there
is dialogue there is subtext.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.680
And where there is action there is intention
of the character (the character’s intentions

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.300
behind their actions).

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:44.150
And where there is the plot line there is
the theme line.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.319
So the plot line answers the question “What
is the story about?”

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:51.220
The theme line answers the question “What
does the story mean?”

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:55.210
And that is the psychological journey compared
to the psychical journey.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:04:00.520
And so that conversation that is constantly
floating around that is up there in that top

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:04.950
realm and plot line, structure as plot.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.630
You need to get down in that other part.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:12.450
That is where the conversation needs to be
having.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:16.019
Moreover (as I said upfront), I think that
is where you start the conversation.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.799
That is your dialogue with the story is with
the characters.

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:23.389
I don’t care what paradigm they use.

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:27.900
If they started with the characters and immersed
themselves in the lives of those characters

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.449
more often than not they are going to come
up with the story structure out of that and

00:04:31.449 --> 00:04:33.930
those characters are going to be alive and
vibrant.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:42.379
So again the answer I think is that people
are not comfortable with that sort of engagement

00:04:42.379 --> 00:04:47.560
with characters on an existential level where
you believe they exist and now I am asking

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:53.229
you to get inside their heads and talk to
them or engage them in conversation.

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:57.330
I think for many people that is something
they feel uncomfortable with.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.569
If they can say “Oh yeah, they need to have
the break in Act 2 on page 25.

00:05:01.569 --> 00:05:03.330
It can’t be between page 23.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:04.330
It can’t be page 29.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:05.689
It’s got to be 25.”

00:05:05.689 --> 00:05:10.610
They are more comfortable with that because
it makes it simpler.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:15.229
Film Courage: So are there no great writers,
just great rewriters?

00:05:15.229 --> 00:05:17.759
I’m sure sure of that is one of your quotes?

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:20.270
Scott: No…it’s writing is rewriting.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:27.020
That’s probably more true of screenwriting
than maybe any other narrative form because

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.309
there are so many layers to get it movie made.

00:05:30.309 --> 00:05:36.539
There is You as the writer and so you get
feedback from your writer friends (you know,

00:05:36.539 --> 00:05:37.629
the writers’ group).

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:41.870
You are represented, you get feedback from
your managers and sometimes your agents depending

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:45.539
upon what your relationship is with your agents.

00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:48.919
Then if you’re working with a producer,
you get feedback from the producer.

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:52.289
And if you’ve got it set up at a studio
or production company or financier, you get

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:54.009
feedback from them.

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:57.479
Then if the thing gets green lit, now you’ve
got actors and directors attached.

00:05:57.479 --> 00:06:01.479
Well now you’re going to have feedback from
the director, feedback from each of the individual

00:06:01.479 --> 00:06:07.330
actors, sometimes they have their own writers
and then you have to synthesize what the other

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.689
writers have done to their characters.

00:06:09.689 --> 00:06:15.279
And so for example I know Eric Heisserer quite
well and he wrote ARRIVAL which is absolutely

00:06:15.279 --> 00:06:18.150
one of the all time fantastic scripts.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.569
And I asked him, I said “How many drafts
did you do?”

00:06:20.569 --> 00:06:22.809
And he said “One hundred.”

00:06:22.809 --> 00:06:30.930
Now most of those were like changing a line
but he would codify each one just so he knew.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:35.479
Or dropping a scene or adding to scenes or
whatever.

00:06:35.479 --> 00:06:39.249
So I think probably out of that I think 10
or 15 drafts.

00:06:39.249 --> 00:06:45.710
But yes, I tell my University students when
they’ve gone through all of the work of

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:52.409
developing a story and then they sit down
and write a draft of it I say “Okay, you

00:06:52.409 --> 00:06:54.379
just have to understand it’s not going to
be perfect.

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:56.059
You’re going to have to rewrite it.”

00:06:56.059 --> 00:06:57.759
And they go “Oh really?”

00:06:57.759 --> 00:07:00.000
They are not used to that.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.539
Talk to them two or three years later, they
completely understand.

00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:04.879
But yes, writing is rewriting.

